### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants accountability, composure, and a systemic fix. Everyone makes mistakes; the question is whether you own it, contain it calmly, and build a guardrail so it cannot repeat.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class MistakeTest {
    enum Signal { MATURE, DEFENSIVE }

    public static Signal evaluate(boolean ownedIt, boolean contained, boolean builtGuardrail) {
        return (ownedIt && contained && builtGuardrail) ? Signal.MATURE : Signal.DEFENSIVE;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // MATURE
    }
}
```

### How do you contain the blast radius?

Stop any further processing, identify exactly which accounts were affected, and revert them using audit records.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class BlastRadiusContainment {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(BlastRadiusContainment.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Step 1: Stop further processing of bulk update");
        log.info("Step 2: Identify affected accounts from audit log");
        log.info("Step 3: Revert wrong interest flag using audit records");
        log.info("Blast radius contained: 150 accounts corrected");
    }
}
```

### What was the actual mistake?

A bulk update to correct an interest flag had a slightly too broad filter, touching more accounts than intended at National Bank of India. The update went straight to production with no preview and no second check.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TheMistake {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The filter was too broad
        String intendedFilter = "WHERE account_type = 'SAVINGS' AND region = 'NORTH'";
        String actualFilter   = "WHERE account_type = 'SAVINGS'"; // missing region!
        System.out.println("Intended: " + intendedFilter);
        System.out.println("Actual:   " + actualFilter);
        System.out.println("Result: touched ALL savings accounts, not just North region");
    }
}
```

### Why is a systemic guardrail better than a personal promise?

"I will double-check next time" is weak because it depends on human memory. "Every bulk update now requires a dry run and a second approval" is strong because it is a system.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DryRunGuardrail {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(DryRunGuardrail.class.getName());

    record AccountChange(String accountId, String before, String after) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Dry run: show what WOULD change without applying
        List<AccountChange> preview = List.of(
            new AccountChange("SAV2001", "REGULAR", "PREMIUM"),
            new AccountChange("SAV2002", "REGULAR", "PREMIUM"),
            new AccountChange("SAV9999", "REGULAR", "PREMIUM") // unexpected!
        );
        log.info("DRY RUN: " + preview.size() + " accounts would change:");
        preview.forEach(c -> log.info("  " + c.accountId() + ": " + c.before() + " -> " + c.after()));
        log.warning("SAV9999 is unexpected! Filter too broad. Aborting.");
    }
}
```

```text
DRY RUN: 3 accounts would change:
  SAV2001: REGULAR -> PREMIUM
  SAV2002: REGULAR -> PREMIUM
  SAV9999: REGULAR -> PREMIUM
SAV9999 is unexpected! Filter too broad. Aborting.
```

### How do you communicate the mistake honestly?

Keep your manager and the operations team informed at every step. In a bank, hiding a production mistake is far worse than the mistake itself.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class HonestCommunication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("[10:05] Discovered: bulk update filter was too broad");
        System.out.println("[10:10] Contained: stopped processing, identified 150 affected accounts");
        System.out.println("[10:25] Corrected: all 150 accounts reverted to correct flag");
        System.out.println("[10:30] Informed manager and ops with full timeline");
    }
}
```

### What is the permanent fix?

Every bulk update now runs a mandatory dry run first. The actual change only applies after a second person explicitly confirms the preview.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BulkUpdateProcess {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("New process for bulk data updates:");
        System.out.println("  1. Write the update query");
        System.out.println("  2. Run DRY RUN: see exactly which accounts change");
        System.out.println("  3. SECOND PERSON reviews and confirms the preview");
        System.out.println("  4. Only then: apply the change");
        System.out.println("  A wrong filter gets caught in step 2, not in production.");
    }
}
```

### How do you show ownership without being self-flagellating?

State the mistake factually, own it plainly, and spend most of the answer on the fix and prevention. Do not dwell on guilt; the interviewer wants to see competence, not suffering.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class OwnershipBalance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Time allocation in answer:");
        System.out.println("  20% - What happened (factual, owned)");
        System.out.println("  30% - How I contained and fixed it");
        System.out.println("  50% - The systemic prevention I built");
        System.out.println("Focus: competence and learning, not guilt");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What if you had not caught it?" (describe the impact and why the guardrail prevents that), "Did the team adopt the process?" (yes, for all bulk operations), and "What was your manager's reaction?" (appreciated honesty and the systemic fix).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class MistakeFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Not caught? -> Wrong flags on 10K accounts, regulatory risk");
        System.out.println("Team adopted? -> Yes, dry-run-first is now standard for all bulk ops");
        System.out.println("Manager reaction? -> Appreciated honesty + the systemic fix");
    }
}
```
